Original commit body from b16fb2b5d77e92bbf1790e16b30b8472cc6bffe0:

verify-claims aborted with RecursionError on keda and exited 1 with an EMPTY
STDOUT -- and exit 1 is also what VIOLATED returns. A CI gate written
`verify-claims ... || exit 1` could not tell "claims violated" from "the
analysis crashed and examined nothing", and a JSON consumer got a zero-byte
file instead of a verdict list. That is the certifying-what-it-did-not-establish
failure INV-javam and INV-fibis were filed for, arriving through the error path
instead of the verdict path, which is why this is an invariant and not a bug
report.

CAUSE. ddg_build._walk_functions was a pure pre-order traversal that recursed
once per child, so Python stack depth WAS tree-sitter AST depth -- one frame per
level, against CPython's default limit of 1000. Machine-generated code exceeds
that without being pathological: keda's vendor/go.temporal.io/api/
workflowservice/v1/request_response.pb.go (725,832 bytes of generated protobuf)
measures an AST DEPTH OF 1171, because one const is a string built as
"..." + "..." + "..." 1,165 TIMES and + is left-associative, giving a 1,165-deep
left spine. Deepest path:
    source_file / const_declaration / const_spec / expression_list
      / binary_expression x1165 / interpreted_string_literal

FIX: an explicit stack. EXACTLY EQUIVALENT, and both halves are checked rather
than asserted -- there is no work after the child loop, so the recursion carried
no state a worklist cannot hold and no post-order step; children are pushed
REVERSED so pop() yields them left to right and visit order (which `out`
accumulates in) is preserved.

sys.setrecursionlimit was rejected: it trades a catchable RecursionError for a
C-stack segfault, strictly worse for a failure already indistinguishable from a
verdict.

MEASURED, on the repo that produced the crash:
    before   rc=1    0 bytes    RecursionError at ddg_build.py:206
    after    rc=1    9,700 bytes    6 verdicts (2 violated, 4 inconclusive)
The exit code is UNCHANGED in both, which is precisely why the fix was needed.

NON-DESTRUCTION asserted, not assumed: an A/B on poetry (Python, DDG-active)
with arms differing only in this hunk is BYTE-IDENTICAL.

IT IS A CLASS, AND THE CLASS IS SIZED. An AST sweep finds 72 self-recursive
child-loop walks across core and all three language packages. A corpus
AST-depth census (files >= 60KB, measured ITERATIVELY so the instrument cannot
hit the bug it measures) puts the realized exposure narrowly:

    repo        scanned   depth>=500   depth>=900   worst
    keda            390            8            7    1171
    dash.js          20            0            0      74
    caddy             2            0            0      37
    mitmproxy         5            0            0      79

All seven keda offenders are generated Go under vendor/; six are
golang.org/x/text/unicode/norm/tables*.go at 947-948 -- WITHIN 5% OF THE LIMIT,
so this is not one pathological file. `survey` over the same input does not
crash and the Go analyzer parses the offending file fine (3,551 symbols), so the
DDG walk was the single live offender. The remaining 71 are filed on the item
rather than rewritten wholesale, since none has a measured failure.

Note _SKIP_DIRS excludes node_modules and .venv but NOT vendor/, which is why
the vendored Go tree is walked at all. Filed as scope, not changed here.

TEST NOTE for whoever extends this: _DDG_LANGUAGES holds only python until the
force-imports inside _build_ddg_for_verify_claims run, and the def/use extractor
registers on IMPORT from a language package. A test that skips either walks
nothing and passes vacuously; the new file guards both, the same way
test_ddg_build.py::test_python_still_produces_edges does.

